data.content:
Here's what was generated:

**`Clock.cs`** — an ambient context backed by `AsyncLocal<T>`, so parallel tests can't interfere with each other. Production cost is one null-check per call.

**Usage in production code** (already applied to `InvoiceCalculator`):
```csharp
Clock.UtcNow   // instead of DateTimeOffset.UtcNow
```

**Usage in tests:**
```csharp
var pinned = new DateTimeOffset(2026, 7, 1, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero);
using (Clock.Override(pinned))
{
    Assert.True(calculator.IsOverdue(pinned.AddDays(-1)));
    Assert.False(calculator.IsOverdue(pinned.AddDays(1)));
}
// real clock is restored automatically after Dispose
```

No DI container, no interfaces, no NuGet packages — just a one-file drop-in. If you later move to .NET 8+ with DI, you can swap this for the built-in `TimeProvider`.
